---
import Guide from "../../layouts/Guide.astro"
import {
  Callout,
  CalloutContent,
  Card,
  CardContent,
  CardHeader,
} from "opui-css/astro"
import AstroLogo from "../../components/AstroLogo.astro"
import DocLink from "../../components/DocLink.astro"
import Html5Logo from "../../components/Html5Logo.astro"
import ReactLogo from "../../components/ReactLogo.astro"
import SvelteLogo from "../../components/SvelteLogo.astro"
import VueLogo from "../../components/VueLogo.astro"

const elements = ["button", "details", "dialog", "input"]
const installs = ["npm i opui-css", "<link>", "button.css"]
const logos = [Html5Logo, AstroLogo, ReactLogo, SvelteLogo, VueLogo]
const looks = [
  { font: "inherit", hue: 30, radius: "var(--ui-radius-round)" },
  { font: "var(--ui-font-code)", hue: 145, radius: "0" },
  { font: "ui-serif, Georgia, serif", hue: 300, radius: "0.5rem" },
]
---

<Guide overline="Guide">
  <Fragment slot="title">Why Open Props UI?</Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="preamble">
    Components built from the HTML and CSS the platform ships today, as files
    you can read, copy and own.
  </Fragment>

  <section id="at-a-glance">
    <div class="pillars">
      <Card variant="outlined">
        <div class="pillar-art ui-not-rich-text" aria-hidden="true">
          {elements.map((element) => <code>&lt;{element}&gt;</code>)}
        </div>
        <CardHeader>
          <h3>Native HTML</h3>
        </CardHeader>
        <CardContent>
          <p>
            A component is a class on the element the platform already has, with
            its behavior and accessibility built in.
          </p>
        </CardContent>
      </Card>
      <Card variant="outlined">
        <div class="pillar-art ui-not-rich-text" aria-hidden="true">
          {installs.map((install, index) => <Fragment>
            {index > 0 && <span class="pillar-or">or</span>}
            <code>{install}</code>
          </Fragment>)}
        </div>
        <CardHeader>
          <h3>You're in charge</h3>
        </CardHeader>
        <CardContent>
          <p>
            Install it from npm, link it from a CDN or copy the files into your
            repo. Take all of it or only what you use. See
            <DocLink href="/guide/getting-started">Getting started</DocLink>.
          </p>
        </CardContent>
      </Card>
      <Card variant="outlined">
        <div class="pillar-art ui-not-rich-text" aria-hidden="true">
          {logos.map((Logo) => (
            <Logo size={24} />
          ))}
        </div>
        <CardHeader>
          <h3>Works with your stack</h3>
        </CardHeader>
        <CardContent>
          <p>
            It's CSS on plain HTML, so anything that renders HTML can use it.
            Astro, React, Svelte and Vue also get typed components.
          </p>
        </CardContent>
      </Card>
      <Card variant="outlined">
        <div class="pillar-art ui-not-rich-text" aria-hidden="true">
          {looks.map(({ font, hue, radius }) => <span
            class="pillar-look ui-palette"
            style={`--ui-palette-hue: ${hue}; --ui-radius: ${radius}; font-family: ${font}`}
          >
            <span class="ui-button ui-filled ui-primary">Save</span>
          </span>)}
        </div>
        <CardHeader>
          <h3>Make it yours</h3>
        </CardHeader>
        <CardContent>
          <p>
            Every component follows your colors, density, corners and fonts, so
            it looks like your product, not like a library. Try the
            <DocLink href="/theme-builder">Theme builder</DocLink>.
          </p>
        </CardContent>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="backstory">
    <h2>Backstory</h2>
    <p>
      When I started this project it was a reaction to UI libraries being slow
      to adopt widespread CSS features. Some barely used CSS grid or
      <code>gap</code>. Open Props UI would be that blank slate rethink of how a
      UI library could be built if you used the most modern features.
    </p>
    <p>
      In general however, I'm mostly just very curious about how far we can push
      HTML and CSS now that so much has happened these last couple of years. For
      understandable reasons many developers haven't been keeping up, but our
      toolbox has been updated so much now that
      <strong>we need examples</strong> of how to use it. Making small demos on
      Codepen or looking at examples on MDN wouldn't cut it - there needed to be
      some weight behind it. I needed to convince myself as much as anyone else
      that these newly landed features are capable of real world usage.
    </p>
    <p>
      As I've been working on this I have gotten more convinced that right now,
      <DocLink href="/guide/browser-support">and going forwards</DocLink>,
      frontend developers need to re-examine their assumptions. If nothing else,
      library developers have to look more closely at what the platform offers.
      AI has pushed developers into an architect kind of role, so if we're not
      as involved in how the code is written anymore, we need to make sure the
      foundations are solid.
    </p>
    <p>
      Or what do I know, maybe the output is
      <a
        href="https://youtu.be/RaQdd8bUaXk?si=SILoMZQZxfyoo-_A&t=657"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >the only thing that matters?</a
      >
      I don't think I believe that yet, but if nothing else this has been fun.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="the-goal">
    <h2>The goal of OPUI</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Have pragmatic defaults without sacrificing flexibility and control
      </li>
      <li>Leverage the native platform as much as possible</li>
      <li>Follow accessibility guidelines</li>
      <li>Serve as an inspiration</li>
      <li>Be scalable</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <div class="ui-not-rich-text">
    <Callout>
      <CalloutContent>
        <h3>
          "If you only use the latest features, that means this can never enter
          production"
        </h3>
        <p>
          Yes and no. Components use a new feature as soon as a browser ships
          it, whichever browser that is. Browsers without it fall back to what
          they do by default, or to a fallback from the
          <DocLink href="/guide/browser-support#compat">compat layer</DocLink>,
          which is included unless you leave it out. If your users' browsers
          have the features, or those fallbacks are good enough, it's ready. The
          point is to let the spec and the components mature alongside each
          other.
        </p>
        <p>We're all adults here, we make the decisions we think are best.</p>
      </CalloutContent>
    </Callout>
  </div>
</Guide>

<style>
  .pillars {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--size-3);
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 20rem), 1fr));
    margin-block-start: var(--size-5);

    h3 {
      font-size: var(--ui-font-size-h6);
      margin: 0;
    }

    p {
      color: var(--ui-text);
      font-size: var(--ui-font-size-small);
      margin: 0;
    }
  }

  .pillar-art {
    align-items: center;
    background-color: var(--ui-surface-tonal);
    border-radius: var(--ui-radius-small);
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--size-2);
    justify-content: center;
    min-block-size: var(--size-10);
    padding: var(--size-3);

    code {
      font-family: var(--ui-font-code);
      font-size: var(--ui-font-size-small);
    }
  }

  .pillar-look {
    --ui-palette-source: oklch(
      58% calc(0.21 * var(--ui-palette-chroma)) var(--ui-palette-hue)
    );
    --ui-primary-source: light-dark(var(--ui-color-9), var(--ui-color-6));
  }

  .pillar-or {
    color: var(--ui-text);
    font-size: var(--ui-font-size-small);
  }
</style>
